# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""OA-size scenario stories, batch 9: stack family, heap family, intervals, xor. 250+ words each."""

QUESTIONS = [

{"id": "sc-127", "v": "ms-hist", "dom": "great halls", "diff": "H",
 "title": "The Banner Beneath the Beams of Wrenhall",
 "story": "The great hall at Wrenhall Castle is spanned by a row of oak beams, each one hung at a slightly different height above the flagstones, because the hall was built piecemeal across four centuries and the carpenters of each age had their own ideas of what a beam ought to be. The steward of the household, a nervous man named Aldous Pemberley, has been instructed by the lady of the castle to hang a single rectangular banner in celebration of her daughter's betrothal. The banner is a flat sheet that must hang straight down from the beams, its top edge level and fastened along an unbroken run of neighbouring beams, and its bottom edge must clear the floor by a reasonable margin.\n\nThe difficulty is the uneven beams. The banner can only hang as far down from its fastenings as the LOWEST beam among those it spans permits, because a lower beam would otherwise cut across the sheet. The sheet may be as wide as the steward likes, covering as many neighbouring beams as he chooses, but a wide banner is limited in depth by the lowest beam it crosses. The lady has stated that she wants the banner to be as large in area as the hall will allow: width times depth, maximised. Aldous has measured, for each beam in the row from the west wall to the east, the depth available beneath it, in hand-spans.\n\nThe hall's minstrels' gallery is said to be haunted by a lady in grey who sings out of tune. The kitchen servants dislike the steward intensely, and he returns the sentiment. A wild cat has taken up residence in the rafters and has defeated three attempts to dislodge it.\n\nInput: line one gives N, the number of beams. Line two gives N depths available beneath each beam, from west to east. Output: the largest area of any banner, where area is the number of neighbouring beams spanned multiplied by the least depth among them.\n\nConstraints: N up to 10^6; depths up to 10^9. Considering every possible pair of end beams and scanning between them for the lowest is hopeless at this size.",
 "clues": ["an unbroken run of neighbouring beams", "limited in depth by the lowest beam it crosses", "width times depth, maximised", "Considering every possible pair of end beams and scanning between them for the lowest is hopeless"],
 "decoys": ["The hall's minstrels' gallery is said to be haunted by a lady in grey who sings out of tune", "A wild cat has taken up residence in the rafters"],
 "core": "Find the largest rectangle formed by a run of neighbouring bars, where the height is the shortest bar in the run.",
 "gloss": [("betrothal", "an engagement to be married", True), ("piecemeal", "done a little at a time", True), ("flagstones", "large flat paving stones", True), ("minstrels", "medieval musicians", True), ("fastenings", "the points where something is attached", True)],
 "why": {"ms-next": "Finding the next lower beam is a step inside the solution, but the question asks to combine a width and the shortest height into the largest area.", "sw-longest": "The 'rule' here is not a cap but a product to be maximised."},
 "tell": "Area limited by the shortest in a run: for each beam find how far it can stretch left and right before something lower stops it."},

{"id": "sc-128", "v": "ms-match", "dom": "lantern festival", "diff": "M",
 "title": "The Lantern Rites of Yuen Shan",
 "story": "The Lantern Festival of Yuen Shan is held on the fifteenth night of the first moon, and its most revered tradition is the Rite of Opening and Closing. A chief lamplighter walks the length of a long processional avenue carrying a roll of coloured paper strips, and at each station along the way she performs exactly one act: she either opens a lantern of a certain colour, or closes one. The colours are red, gold and jade, and each colour has its own symbol in the festival record: opening is written as the left half of a pair of marks, and closing as the right half. Red uses round marks, gold uses square marks, and jade uses angled marks.\n\nThe temple elders have laid down the following rule. Whenever a lantern is closed, it must be the lantern that was opened MOST RECENTLY among those still open, and it must be of the same colour as the act of closing; for to close a gold lantern while a jade one burns inside it would be a grave discourtesy to the ancestors. By the end of the avenue, every lantern that was opened must have been closed. A record of acts that obeys all these conditions is called a harmonious rite; anything else is a blemish.\n\nA young scribe named Wen has been asked to check dozens of festival records, each a string of marks, and say for each whether it is a harmonious rite. The scribe's brush, unfortunately, has been dipped in the wrong ink, and some of the marks are smudged beyond reading, though that is a problem for the next paragraph and not this one. The lamplighters' guild holds a banquet after the festival at which an enormous dumpling is served.\n\nInput: the first line gives T, the number of records. Each of the next T lines gives a string made of the characters ( ) [ ] { }. Output: for each record, YES if it is a harmonious rite, NO otherwise.\n\nConstraints: T up to 10^4; each record up to 10^6 characters. Wen would like to read each record once from left to right and decide as he goes, without rewriting the record or looking back more than the most recent unclosed act.",
 "clues": ["it must be the lantern that was opened MOST RECENTLY among those still open", "it must be of the same colour as the act of closing", "every lantern that was opened must have been closed", "read each record once from left to right and decide as he goes"],
 "decoys": ["The lamplighters' guild holds a banquet after the festival at which an enormous dumpling is served", "The scribe's brush, unfortunately, has been dipped in the wrong ink"],
 "core": "Check that three kinds of paired marks are properly nested: each closing mark must match the latest unclosed opening one.",
 "gloss": [("revered", "greatly respected", True), ("processional", "relating to a ceremonial march", True), ("discourtesy", "rudeness", True), ("harmonious", "fitting together well", True), ("blemish", "a flaw", True)],
 "why": {"ms-greedy": "No building of a best result is going on; it is a validity check on nesting.", "td-up": "No tree is given, although nesting is tree-like."},
 "tell": "Last opened, first closed, and everything must be closed by the end: remember the unclosed openers and check each closer against the latest."},

{"id": "sc-129", "v": "ms-greedy", "dom": "lock museum", "diff": "H",
 "title": "The Combination of the Strongroom at Tolbrook",
 "story": "The lock museum at Tolbrook exhibits the strongroom of a Victorian bank, preserved with every bolt and lever in place, and its most treasured item is a leather-bound manual that records the combination in a long string of digits. For reasons of insurance, the museum's director, a fastidious man called Barnabas Quill, is obliged to reduce the displayed combination to a shorter, harmless figure, so that no visitor may use it. The rule agreed with the insurers is exact: the director must strike out exactly K digits from the string, leaving the remaining digits in their ORIGINAL left-to-right order, with no digit moved from its place relative to the others.\n\nBarnabas has his own aesthetic preference, however. Of all the figures he could leave behind by striking out K digits, he wants the numerically smallest, because a small number looks tidy on the display card and because, he says, it is more insulting to a would-be thief. If the remaining digits begin with zeros, those leading zeros are simply dropped when reading the figure as a number, and if everything is struck out, or only zeros remain, the figure is read as 0. He needs to know what that smallest figure is, as a string of digits without leading zeros.\n\nThe museum is conveniently situated next to a railway station, and the passing trains rattle the cases. A volunteer guide wears a deerstalker hat, which visitors find either charming or tiresome. The gift shop sells replica keys, none of which open anything. The strongroom's famous scratches on the floor were left by a clerk who dragged a safe across it in 1893.\n\nInput: line one gives N, the number of digits, and K. Line two gives the string of N digits. Output: the smallest number obtainable after striking out exactly K digits, without leading zeros (print 0 if the result is empty or all zeros).\n\nConstraints: N up to 10^6; K between 0 and N. Trying every possible choice of K digits to strike out is impossible; and since the digits must stay in order, the director wants a single left-to-right pass, deciding each time whether the digit just read ought to push out a larger digit read earlier.",
 "clues": ["strike out exactly K digits from the string, leaving the remaining digits in their ORIGINAL left-to-right order", "the numerically smallest", "a single left-to-right pass", "whether the digit just read ought to push out a larger digit read earlier"],
 "decoys": ["The museum is conveniently situated next to a railway station", "A volunteer guide wears a deerstalker hat"],
 "core": "Remove exactly K digits from a number, keeping order, so that the result is as small as possible.",
 "gloss": [("fastidious", "very particular about details", True), ("strongroom", "a secure room for valuables", True), ("aesthetic", "concerned with beauty", True), ("insurers", "companies that cover losses for a fee", True), ("lever", "a bar that pivots to move something", True)],
 "why": {"ms-next": "Next-smaller relationships are used inside, but the question is about building a final smallest figure by dropping digits.", "dp-linear": "A table over positions would work but is heavier than the single-pass way the director asks for."},
 "tell": "Build the smallest result left to right, discarding a previous bigger digit whenever a smaller one arrives and removals remain."},

{"id": "sc-130", "v": "hp-topk", "dom": "pearl diving", "diff": "M",
 "title": "The Heaviest Pearls of the Aurelian Banks",
 "story": "Along the Aurelian Banks, in the warm shallows of the southern sea, the pearl divers of the Marisol cooperative work from dawn until the sun climbs too high to see beneath the surface. Every pearl brought up is weighed on the cooperative's brass scales at the quay by the weighmaster, an elderly woman called Catalina Ruiz, and its weight in grains is shouted aloud and noted by a clerk. Pearls arrive one at a time, continually, over the long days of the season, and an astonishing number of them pass the scales: tens of millions, if the cooperative's boast is to be believed.\n\nThe cooperative has a contract with the royal jeweller to deliver, at the end of the season, the K heaviest pearls that were found, K being a smallish number such as fifty. Unfortunately, the cooperative has no record of every weight: the clerk's notebook holds room for only a few hundred entries and was long since filled and thrown away, and the quay has no warehouse for pearls that are not among the best. At any moment during the season, the weighmaster must be able to answer the question 'which are the K heaviest pearls seen so far, and what is the lightest of these?' because the jeweller's agent likes to drop in and ask. She will be told each new weight just once, as it is shouted aloud, and she cannot ask for it to be repeated.\n\nThe quay is overrun with gulls, who have a taste for the divers' lunches. A rival cooperative to the east is thought to be selling coloured glass beads to credulous tourists. Catalina's grandson sells coconut sweets from a barrow by the customs shed.\n\nInput: line one gives N, the number of pearls weighed and K. Line two gives the N weights in the order they were announced. Output: the K greatest weights seen, in decreasing order.\n\nConstraints: N up to 10^7; K up to 100; weights up to 10^9. Memory may grow with K, not with N. Sorting the entire stream at the end is not allowed, as the stream has already gone by.",
 "clues": ["the K heaviest pearls that were found, K being a smallish number", "the clerk's notebook holds room for only a few hundred entries", "She will be told each new weight just once", "Memory may grow with K, not with N"],
 "decoys": ["The quay is overrun with gulls, who have a taste for the divers' lunches", "Catalina's grandson sells coconut sweets from a barrow by the customs shed"],
 "core": "From a long stream, keep only the K largest values, using memory proportional to K.",
 "gloss": [("grains", "a small unit of weight used for pearls", True), ("weighmaster", "an official who weighs goods", True), ("cooperative", "a business owned jointly by its members", True), ("credulous", "too ready to believe", True), ("quay", "a landing place for ships", True)],
 "why": {"hp-two": "Balancing two halves is for a middle value; here only the top K matter.", "bs-answer": "No threshold is being searched for."},
 "tell": "A running record of the best K from a stream: hold K candidates and, for each new arrival, evict the weakest if it loses."},

{"id": "sc-131", "v": "hp-merge", "dom": "river gauges", "diff": "H",
 "title": "The Gauge Stations of the Severn Weir",
 "story": "The Water Board's hydrological office at Severn Weir receives readings from a chain of gauge stations strung along a river basin, each of which keeps its own logbook of water level. Every station writes in its logbook in strict order of time, earliest reading first, and the logbooks are mailed to the office in the form of long telex strips, one per station. The office wants a single chronological record, covering every reading from every station, in order of the time at which the reading was taken, so that the engineers can study the passage of a flood wave from one station to the next.\n\nThe complication is the telex machinery. There are K stations, K being at most a couple of thousand. The office's machine can only pull one reading at a time from each strip, from its current end, and it cannot go back; nor can it hold on the desk more than one reading from each strip, for a strip that has been fed in can never be unspooled again. The office's stock of paper is limited, so the combined record must be produced as the readings come, writing each line to the final record as soon as the next one in time order is known, without copying all the strips into one pile and sorting that pile afterwards.\n\nThe river basin includes a famous salmon ladder, which attracts tourists, and a weir whose roar can be heard for a mile. The head hydrologist, Gwendolen Rees, owns three spaniels, all of which accompany her to work. The canteen's rice pudding is considered a local delicacy, though those who dislike it say so only in whispers.\n\nInput: line one gives K. Each of the next K lines begins with a count M_i followed by M_i timestamps in increasing order. Output: all the timestamps from all stations in one non-decreasing sequence.\n\nConstraints: K up to 2000; total readings up to 5 * 10^6; timestamps up to 10^9. The office would like to keep in view only one reading per station at any moment, and to select the earliest among those K in good time, rather than compare each new reading against all the others.",
 "clues": ["Every station writes in its logbook in strict order of time", "a single chronological record, covering every reading from every station", "it hold on the desk more than one reading from each strip", "select the earliest among those K in good time, rather than compare each new reading against all the others"],
 "decoys": ["a famous salmon ladder, which attracts tourists", "The head hydrologist, Gwendolen Rees, owns three spaniels"],
 "core": "Merge K sorted lists into one sorted list, holding only the current front item of each list.",
 "gloss": [("hydrological", "relating to water and its movement", True), ("telex", "an old text-message machine on paper strips", True), ("gauge", "an instrument for measuring a quantity", True), ("chronological", "in order of time", False), ("unspooled", "unwound", True)],
 "why": {"tp-merge": "Two sorted sequences can be merged with a cursor each, but with a couple of thousand sources, comparing all fronts each time is slow; something must pick the earliest of many quickly.", "iv-sortkey": "Sorting everything at the end is exactly what the office is forbidden to do."},
 "tell": "Many sorted sources merged into one: keep each source's current front in a structure that always gives the smallest."},

{"id": "sc-132", "v": "hp-two", "dom": "fish market", "diff": "H",
 "title": "The Middle Price at Grimsby Dock",
 "story": "At the fish market on Grimsby Dock, the auction starts before dawn, and the auctioneer, a barrel-chested man named Obadiah Teague, calls out the price per stone for each lot as it is knocked down. The Fisheries Board, anxious to reassure the public that no single bumper catch or freak shortage distorts the picture, publishes a figure called the Middle Price: the price of the lot that would stand exactly in the middle if all lots sold so far that morning were lined up in order of price, or, when an even number of lots have been sold, the average of the two in the middle.\n\nThe Middle Price must be updated after every single lot, because the Board's ticker, mounted on a pole over the dock, shows the figure live. There may be hundreds of thousands of lots in the course of a season's busiest day, and no lot, once sold, is ever withdrawn or changed. Obadiah's clerk, a boy of sixteen, cannot afford to re-sort the whole record of the morning's prices each time a new one arrives, for the ticker would fall hopelessly behind; yet the Board will not accept a figure that is approximate or that omits any lot.\n\nThere is a long and bitter rivalry between the herring merchants and the cod merchants, and fights occasionally break out over the position of a stall. The market's cat, a one-eyed tom named Admiral, patrols the cod stalls with an air of proprietorship. A visiting inspector from the capital has complained of the smell, which is regarded locally as a compliment.\n\nInput: line one gives N, the number of lots. Line two gives the N prices in the order they were sold. Output: N numbers, the Middle Price after each lot, each to one decimal place.\n\nConstraints: N up to 5 * 10^5; prices up to 10^9. Each update should cost only a small, slowly growing amount of effort, and the method must be able to give the middle at any moment without holding the prices in sorted order or re-sorting them.",
 "clues": ["the price of the lot that would stand exactly in the middle", "must be updated after every single lot", "cannot afford to re-sort the whole record", "no lot, once sold, is ever withdrawn"],
 "decoys": ["a long and bitter rivalry between the herring merchants and the cod merchants", "The market's cat, a one-eyed tom named Admiral"],
 "core": "After each new value arrives in a stream, report the median of everything so far.",
 "gloss": [("auction", "a sale where goods go to the highest bidder", True), ("bumper", "unusually large", True), ("proprietorship", "the feeling of owning something", True), ("ticker", "a display that shows changing figures", True), ("approximate", "close but not exact", True)],
 "why": {"hp-topk": "Keeping only the top few discards the lower values, but a median needs both halves kept.", "bs-partition": "That is for two fixed sorted lists, not a stream."},
 "tell": "Running middle of a stream: keep the lower half and upper half in two structures that expose their boundaries, and rebalance."},

{"id": "sc-133", "v": "hp-greedy", "dom": "rope walks", "diff": "M",
 "title": "The Splicing Charges of Carrick Ropewalk",
 "story": "The ropewalk at Carrick is a quarter-mile shed in which hemp is twisted into cable for the naval dockyards, and its master, a taciturn man called Silas Pardoe, has received an unusual order. The Admiralty wants the ropewalk to join a collection of separate lengths of rope into one single continuous cable. The lengths are of various sizes, measured in fathoms, and the ropewalk has them lying in the yard in no particular order.\n\nThe only operation available is splicing, in which exactly two lengths, wherever they lie, are joined end to end into one longer length. Splicing is skilled work, and the guild charges for it by the length it creates: splicing a length of 7 fathoms to one of 4 costs 11 fathoms' worth of effort, the sum of the two, and the resulting length of 11 fathoms then takes its place among the lengths in the yard, to be spliced onward in its turn. Silas may choose the order in which lengths are joined and which two to join at each step, so long as every step joins exactly two lengths and the process ends when only a single length remains.\n\nSilas wants to find the least total charge for turning all the lengths in the yard into one cable. He has noticed that some orders are cheaper than others: joining two huge lengths early means their large size is charged again every time the result is spliced again. He does not wish to try every possible order of joining, for the number of orders is astronomical.\n\nA digression: the ropewalk's walkers, who spin the hemp, walk backwards the whole length of the shed, an ancient habit. The apprentices are fed on bread and dripping. A gull sits on the roof every morning and looks down at the proceedings with unconcealed contempt.\n\nInput: line one gives N, the number of lengths. Line two gives N lengths. Output: the minimum possible total charge.\n\nConstraints: N up to 2 * 10^5; each length up to 10^9; the answer fits in a 64-bit integer. At each step the cheapest move depends only on which two lengths are currently shortest, and the collection changes after every step.",
 "clues": ["exactly two lengths, wherever they lie, are joined end to end into one longer length", "the resulting length of 11 fathoms then takes its place among the lengths in the yard", "the least total charge", "the cheapest move depends only on which two lengths are currently shortest, and the collection changes after every step"],
 "decoys": ["the ropewalk's walkers, who spin the hemp, walk backwards the whole length of the shed", "A gull sits on the roof every morning and looks down at the proceedings with unconcealed contempt"],
 "core": "Repeatedly merge two items at a cost equal to their sum, where the merged item goes back into the collection; minimise the total cost.",
 "gloss": [("ropewalk", "a long building where rope is made", True), ("splicing", "joining ropes by weaving their strands", True), ("fathoms", "units of length for rope and water depth, about 1.8 m", True), ("taciturn", "saying very little", True), ("hemp", "a plant fibre used for rope", True)],
 "why": {"gr-local": "A one-pass greedy over a fixed order does not apply; the choice changes as merged items re-enter the collection.", "dp-interval": "Joining ranges in a fixed order would be range DP; here lengths may be joined in any order."},
 "tell": "Always combine the two cheapest, and the result goes back in: repeatedly take the two smallest from a structure that gives minimums."},

{"id": "sc-134", "v": "iv-merge", "dom": "canal locks", "diff": "E",
 "title": "The Lock Closures on the Wendover Cut",
 "story": "The Wendover Cut is a stretch of canal winding through the chalk hills for twenty miles, with a flight of locks every few furlongs, and the Canal Trust's chief engineer, a fastidious man named Percival Nankivell, has spent the winter compiling a schedule of maintenance closures. Each closure is a period during which a lock is shut to traffic and the navigation notices must be updated: it begins on a stated day and ends on a stated day, and both the first and last day are included in the closure. The closures have been submitted by a dozen different gangs of workmen, each acting independently, and the list that has landed on Percival's desk is in no sensible order whatever.\n\nThe Trust wishes to publish a short list of 'periods of disruption', each of which is a continuous stretch of days during which at least one closure is in force somewhere on the cut, and in which no day is not covered by at least one closure. Where two closures overlap, or where one begins on the very day after another ends, there is no gap between them, and they must be reported as one single longer period. Two periods are separate only if there is at least one free day between them. The published periods should be given in chronological order.\n\nThe canal has a reputation for the quality of its pub landlords, and every lock has its own inn. The Trust's annual dinner features a roast which no one can identify. The chief engineer's moustache is considered an authority on weather, twitching when rain is imminent.\n\nInput: line one gives N, the number of closures. Each of the next N lines gives two integers, the first day and last day of one closure, in no particular order of closures. Output: the merged periods, each on its own line as two integers, in order of first day.\n\nConstraints: N up to 10^6; days between 1 and 10^9; the first day of each closure is not later than its last day. The Trust wants the work to stay close to the effort of ordering the list, not to the square of its length.",
 "clues": ["Where two closures overlap, or where one begins on the very day after another ends", "they must be reported as one single longer period", "the list that has landed on Percival's desk is in no sensible order whatever", "Two periods are separate only if there is at least one free day between them"],
 "decoys": ["The canal has a reputation for the quality of its pub landlords", "The chief engineer's moustache is considered an authority on weather"],
 "core": "Combine overlapping or touching ranges of days into continuous periods, given in no particular order.",
 "gloss": [("furlong", "an eighth of a mile", True), ("navigation", "the movement of boats along waterways", True), ("closure", "a period when something is shut", True), ("continuous", "unbroken", False), ("fastidious", "very particular about details", True)],
 "why": {"iv-sweep": "A count of simultaneous closures is not wanted; the question is for the merged periods themselves.", "iv-greedy": "Choosing a largest compatible set is a different question."},
 "tell": "Sort the ranges by start, then extend the current block while the next range begins within or right after it."},

{"id": "sc-135", "v": "iv-insert", "dom": "hot springs", "diff": "M",
 "title": "The Bath Bookings of Bad Winterau",
 "story": "The spa town of Bad Winterau has a grand bathhouse fed by natural hot springs, and its booking clerk, Hildegard Roth, keeps the reservation book for the private thermal suite. Each reservation in the book occupies the suite from a stated start hour to a stated end hour, counted in hours from the beginning of the month, and the bookings are entered in order of start hour. The bathhouse has a firm rule that no two reservations may overlap, and the book has been carefully kept so that all the reservations in it are already separated from one another by gaps of at least a little, and are already in order. Hildegard has never had to sort it, and she is proud of this.\n\nA distinguished guest, a retired admiral, has now asked for a block of time in the suite, from hour S to hour E. His booking is welcome, but with a condition: the admiral's presence takes precedence over any reservation that clashes with it. Rather than turn away the guests whose slots overlap his, the bathhouse's custom is to combine them: any existing reservations that overlap the admiral's block, or touch it, are absorbed with it into one single, longer reservation, running from the earliest start among them to the latest end among them. Reservations that do not touch his block are left untouched.\n\nHildegard must produce the updated book with the admiral's block inserted. The book is kept as a printed ledger with no room to spare, and she hates to copy it afresh, and particularly dislikes sorting it, since it was kept in order by the clerk before her. The book is long, having been kept for many years.\n\nThe town has a famous brass band that parades on Sundays, and the bathhouse serves a rather watery coffee in the lobby. The admiral is said to snore like a foghorn.\n\nInput: line one gives N and then the admiral's S and E. The next N lines each give a reservation's start and end hour, already sorted by start and with no two overlapping. Output: the updated list of reservations, one per line, in order.\n\nConstraints: N up to 10^6; hours up to 10^9. A single sweep along the existing ledger, with no sorting, is expected.",
 "clues": ["all the reservations in it are already separated from one another by gaps of at least a little, and are already in order", "any existing reservations that overlap the admiral's block, or touch it, are absorbed with it into one single, longer reservation", "Reservations that do not touch his block are left untouched", "dislikes sorting it"],
 "decoys": ["The town has a famous brass band that parades on Sundays", "The admiral is said to snore like a foghorn"],
 "core": "Insert one new range into a sorted list of non-overlapping ranges, merging whatever it touches, in one pass.",
 "gloss": [("thermal", "relating to heat", True), ("precedence", "priority over others", True), ("absorbed", "taken in and made part of", True), ("clash", "to conflict", True), ("distinguished", "highly respected", True)],
 "why": {"iv-merge": "That is for an unsorted pile of ranges; here the ledger is already ordered and disjoint, so no sorting is needed.", "iv-sweep": "No counting of overlap is involved."},
 "tell": "A sorted disjoint list plus one new range: copy those that end before it, merge those that overlap it, copy the rest."},

{"id": "sc-136", "v": "iv-greedy", "dom": "carnival", "diff": "M",
 "title": "The Contests of the Midsummer Carnival",
 "story": "The Midsummer Carnival at Kellerby runs for a single day, and its programme lists a bewildering number of contests: pie-eating, tug-of-war, a greasy-pole climb, an egg-and-spoon race, a baking competition, a dog show, and many others. Each contest has a published start time and a published finish time, counted in minutes from the opening of the gates. The programme is printed in no order that makes any sense; it was assembled by a committee, and the printer set the contests in the order in which the committee shouted them out.\n\nA famous judge, Dame Cordelia Fawkes, has been invited to attend as many of the contests as she can. Her rules are strict. She will watch a contest from beginning to end or not at all; she can only be at one contest at a time; and she can move from the end of one contest to the start of another at no cost, so that a contest beginning at the exact minute another ends is still attendable. Dame Cordelia wants to know the greatest number of contests she can attend in a day, though she does not much care which ones, as long as the number is as large as possible.\n\nDame Cordelia's reputation for fairness is such that the carnival committee dare not make a mistake. The committee chairman, a stout man named Horatio Pym, has already made a scene about the placement of the bunting. A brass band plays on the green, and the beer tent is doing a roaring trade. Rain is forecast for the late afternoon.\n\nInput: line one gives N, the number of contests. Each of the next N lines gives the start minute and finish minute of one contest. Output: the largest number of contests that can be attended without any two overlapping.\n\nConstraints: N up to 10^6; minutes up to 10^9; the start is strictly before the finish. A tempting plan is to take the contest that starts earliest, or the one that is shortest, but both can lead a judge astray; the committee has also made clear that trying every combination of contests is out of the question.",
 "clues": ["She will watch a contest from beginning to end or not at all", "she can only be at one contest at a time", "the greatest number of contests she can attend", "take the contest that starts earliest, or the one that is shortest, but both can lead a judge astray"],
 "decoys": ["The committee chairman, a stout man named Horatio Pym, has already made a scene about the placement of the bunting", "A brass band plays on the green, and the beer tent is doing a roaring trade"],
 "core": "Choose the largest set of time ranges that do not overlap each other.",
 "gloss": [("bewildering", "confusing because of the number or variety", True), ("attendable", "able to be attended", True), ("programme", "a list of events", True), ("contest", "a competition", True), ("astray", "off the right course", False)],
 "why": {"iv-sweep": "Counting how many overlap at once is a different question; here we pick a set that has no overlap.", "dp-knapsack": "Each contest has equal value, so weighted selection is unnecessary."},
 "tell": "Maximum number of non-clashing ranges: sort by finishing time and keep each one that starts after the last chosen finish."},

{"id": "sc-137", "v": "iv-sweep", "dom": "quarantine island", "diff": "M",
 "title": "The Beds of Saint Elmo's Lazaretto",
 "story": "Off the harbour of Port Calder lies the small island of Saint Elmo, where for four centuries the port's ships have discharged sick crews and suspect cargo into a quarantine hospital known as the Lazaretto. The steward of the Lazaretto, a methodical woman named Beatrix Salmon, is planning a renovation and needs to know how many beds the island must be able to supply at the busiest moment in its recent history.\n\nFrom the register of the last ten years, Beatrix has extracted, for each patient, two numbers: the day on which they were admitted and the day on which they were discharged. Each patient occupied exactly one bed, from the whole of their admission day to the whole of their discharge day, both included. A patient discharged on day 40 and another admitted on day 40 therefore needed two separate beds that day, because the first had not yet left. There are tens of thousands of patients in the register, and their records were entered in the order in which the register's clerks happened to reach them, not in any chronological order.\n\nBeatrix does not need to know who occupied the beds, nor how long the average stay lasted. She needs only the largest number of patients present on the island on any single day. The architect has promised to build exactly that many beds, and not one more, to keep within the budget approved by the harbour commissioners.\n\nThe island is home to a colony of ring-tailed lizards, which the patients are encouraged to feed. The Lazaretto's chapel, though tiny, has an altar painting that scholars attribute to a pupil of Titian. Visitors are ferried over on Thursdays by a boatman called Ignatius.\n\nInput: line one gives N, the number of patients. Each of the next N lines gives the admission day and discharge day of one patient. Output: the greatest number of patients present at any one time.\n\nConstraints: N up to 10^6; days up to 10^9, so Beatrix cannot afford a separate tally for each day of the ten years; admission day not later than discharge day. Her inspection of the register must be completed in about the time it takes to put the days in order.",
 "clues": ["from the whole of their admission day to the whole of their discharge day, both included", "the largest number of patients present on the island on any single day", "Beatrix cannot afford a separate tally for each day of the ten years", "A patient discharged on day 40 and another admitted on day 40 therefore needed two separate beds"],
 "decoys": ["The island is home to a colony of ring-tailed lizards", "an altar painting that scholars attribute to a pupil of Titian"],
 "core": "From many time ranges, find the largest number of them that overlap at any single moment.",
 "gloss": [("quarantine", "isolation to prevent disease from spreading", True), ("lazaretto", "a hospital for people with infectious diseases", True), ("methodical", "orderly and systematic", True), ("discharged", "released from hospital", True), ("commissioners", "officials who oversee a matter", True)],
 "why": {"iv-greedy": "A largest non-overlapping selection would pick a set; here all patients are present and the count of overlap is wanted.", "ps-diff": "Day-by-day tallies would work but the day range is far too wide for that."},
 "tell": "Peak simultaneous load: turn every range into an arrival and a departure, sort them, and keep a running count."},

{"id": "sc-138", "v": "iv-sortkey", "dom": "desert caravans", "diff": "H",
 "title": "The Caravans to the Oasis of Qasr Adan",
 "story": "Across the Rub al-Khali there runs a single track to the oasis of Qasr Adan, and on it, one morning, a number of camel caravans set out for the same well. The track is narrow and the dunes on either side are impassable, so a caravan can never overtake another; if a faster caravan catches up with a slower one ahead of it, it must fall in behind and travel at the slower one's pace for the rest of the journey. A caravan that is joined in this way by others becomes part of a single convoy, and a convoy arrives at the oasis as one: all its members reach the well at the same moment, namely the moment of the slowest one among those in front of it.\n\nThe oasis keeper, an old man named Yusuf al-Tamimi, has been told that N caravans started out on the same morning from different points along the track. For each caravan he has the distance it still had to travel from its starting point to the well, and the steady speed at which it would travel if nothing were in its way. A caravan that starts closer to the well is ahead of one that starts further away. Yusuf has no way of seeing the caravans from the well, only these records, and he wants to know how many separate convoys will actually arrive. Caravans that meet exactly at the well count as one convoy.\n\nThe oasis is famous for its dates, which are packed into sweet cakes, and for a small mosque whose minaret leans noticeably to the east. Yusuf's grandson keeps a pet falcon, which sits on a perch and appears to be perpetually offended. The heat in the afternoon is such that even the lizards seek shade.\n\nInput: line one gives N. Each of the next N lines gives, for one caravan, the distance still to travel and its speed. The caravans are listed in no order. Output: the number of separate convoys that arrive at the oasis.\n\nConstraints: N up to 10^6; distances up to 10^9, all distinct; speeds up to 10^6. Simulating the journey minute by minute is out of the question; the question must be answered from the records alone, once they have been put in the proper order.",
 "clues": ["a caravan can never overtake another", "A caravan that starts closer to the well is ahead of one that starts further away", "all its members reach the well at the same moment, namely the moment of the slowest one among those in front of it", "The caravans are listed in no order"],
 "decoys": ["The oasis is famous for its dates, which are packed into sweet cakes", "Yusuf's grandson keeps a pet falcon"],
 "core": "Cars on a road cannot overtake; count how many groups arrive, by ordering them by position and comparing arrival times.",
 "gloss": [("oasis", "a fertile spot in a desert with water", True), ("convoy", "a group of vehicles or animals travelling together", True), ("caravan", "a group of travellers crossing a desert together", True), ("minaret", "a tall tower of a mosque", True), ("impassable", "unable to be crossed", True)],
 "why": {"ms-next": "A structure for nearest-larger could be used, but the heart is ordering by the right key and taking one pass.", "iv-sweep": "No overlap counting of time ranges is involved."},
 "tell": "Order the items by position, then walk from the front deciding who merges into whom by comparing arrival times."},

{"id": "sc-139", "v": "bt-xor", "dom": "loom weaving", "diff": "E",
 "title": "The Punch Cards of the Jacquard Loom",
 "story": "The silk mills of Lyonne still weave their brocades on antique Jacquard looms, in which the pattern is fed to the machine on a long chain of punched cards. Each card carries a number stamped in its margin, and in the mill's tradition every pattern card is made in a pair: a front card and a back card, bearing the identical number, so that the weaver can check one against the other. If a card is lost, the pattern cannot be woven correctly, and every pair is therefore kept in the mill's archive in a great box, shuffled together with every other pair.\n\nThe master weaver, Jacques Montaigu, has been reorganising the archive when he discovers that exactly one of the numbers in the box appears an odd number of times: all the others appear precisely twice, a front card and its back card, but one number appears only once, because the partner of that card has gone missing, probably when the mill was flooded in the spring. He wants to find that number, so that the lost partner can be recut. There are hundreds of thousands of cards, and they are thoroughly mixed. Jacques has no wish to sort them, because he would have to carry the whole box to the cutting room and the mill's floor is already cramped.\n\nThere is a belief among the weavers that the missing card is always the one for the pattern that happens to be in fashion that season. Jacques ignores this superstition. The mill cat is a large tortoiseshell who sleeps in a basket of silk waste. A shipment of raw silk from the east has been delayed.\n\nInput: line one gives N, the number of cards in the box (always odd). Line two gives the N numbers stamped on the cards, in no order. Output: the single number that appears once.\n\nConstraints: N up to 10^6; numbers up to 10^9. Jacques insists on a single pass through the box, with a constant amount of memory: no notes, no tally sheets, and no sorting of the cards. Every other number appears exactly twice.",
 "clues": ["every pattern card is made in a pair", "exactly one of the numbers in the box appears an odd number of times", "a single pass through the box, with a constant amount of memory", "Every other number appears exactly twice"],
 "decoys": ["a belief among the weavers that the missing card is always the one for the pattern that happens to be in fashion", "The mill cat is a large tortoiseshell who sleeps in a basket of silk waste"],
 "core": "Every value occurs twice except one; find the odd one out using constant memory in a single pass.",
 "gloss": [("brocade", "a rich fabric woven with raised patterns", True), ("Jacquard loom", "a loom controlled by punched cards", True), ("antique", "very old", True), ("reorganising", "arranging in a new order", True), ("superstition", "a belief not based on evidence", True)],
 "why": {"hs-freq": "A tally table would work but uses memory proportional to the number of distinct patterns, which the story forbids.", "hs-seen": "Remembering what has been seen needs extra memory too."},
 "tell": "Pairs that cancel and one that remains, with constant memory: combine all values with a self-cancelling operation."},
]
